# gitleaks — accepted findings
##############################
#
# ONE ENTRY PER FINDING, BY FINGERPRINT. A fingerprint is
# `commit:path:rule:line`, so an entry here suppresses exactly one match at one
# line of one blob and nothing else.
#
# DELIBERATELY NOT USED, and each for the same reason — every one of them would
# suppress findings nobody has looked at, including ones that do not exist yet:
#
#   * no `[allowlist] paths` entry for oncotriage/storage/queries.py — that
#     would hide a real credential added to that file tomorrow;
#   * no `stopwords` or regex allowlist — that would hide the same string
#     anywhere it appears in the repository;
#   * no rule disable for generic-api-key — that rule is the one most likely to
#     catch a real leak in a project whose .env carries an OpenAI key and a
#     Qdrant cloud credential.
#
# The cost of the narrow form is that a rebase or a filter-branch changes the
# commit hash and the entry stops matching, so the finding comes back. That is
# the correct failure direction: it fails loudly and gets re-reviewed, rather
# than staying suppressed against a blob nobody can locate any more.

# oncotriage/storage/queries.py:505 — NOT A CREDENTIAL.
#
# THE OFFENDING LINE IS DESCRIBED HERE, NEVER QUOTED, and that is not fussiness:
# the first version of this file reproduced it verbatim to explain it, and
# gitleaks promptly flagged THIS FILE at the quoted line
# (.gitleaksignore:28, a finding that did not exist before the fix was written).
# The documentation of a suppression must not itself be suppressible. This
# project has hit the same shape before — pass 20e's exec-chain scanner reported
# nine docstrings explaining the defect it was written to catch.
#
# What is at that line: a `Query(...)` record in the query registry, whose first
# keyword argument is named `key` and whose value is the report's identifier —
# a long lower-case snake_case metric name, ~34 characters, made of English
# words joined by underscores. It names one of the ~40 read-only reports in
# oncotriage/storage/queries.py and is printed in that file's own output.
#
# gitleaks' generic-api-key rule fires on that argument NAME next to a
# long-enough string, plus a Shannon entropy of 3.91 — which any long
# underscore-joined identifier clears on length alone.
#
# Checked rather than assumed: the blob at that commit was read, the surrounding
# Query(...) record inspected, and the same construction appears ~40 times in the
# file with only this one crossing the entropy threshold. Nothing at that line
# is secret, rotatable, or usable against any service.
#
# The commit is where the query layer was extracted (item 38); the string is
# unchanged at HEAD, so a future scan of HEAD's blob would need its own entry if
# the rule ever fires there too.
6a029ac2dbae950ee647905733b95f9aecb15397:oncotriage/storage/queries.py:generic-api-key:505

