# tests/test_agent_bedrock_anthropic_per_trial.py:559 at commit 9a3fdb8 —
# A PROMPT-CACHE ROUTING LABEL. NOT A CREDENTIAL.
#
# DESCRIBED, NOT QUOTED, per the lesson recorded above.
#
# What is at that line: the keyword argument that carries Stage 5's prompt-cache
# ROUTING HINT into the warmup call. It asks a provider to send requests sharing
# a prompt prefix to one machine so the cache is hit. Production derives its
# value from the system prompt's digest behind a project prefix; the value in
# that revision was a fabricated label of about twenty characters written for a
# test. It authenticates nothing, it is issued by nobody, it grants no access,
# and there is nothing to rotate. The test never reads the value — only that the
# argument was forwarded at all.
#
# gitleaks' generic-api-key rule fires because the argument NAME ends in `key`
# and the value cleared both the rule's ten-character floor and its entropy
# threshold (measured 3.59 against a threshold of 3.5).
#
# THE SOURCE IS ALREADY FIXED, so this is one entry and not a recurring one. The
# label at HEAD is under ten characters and cannot reach that rule's length
# floor, which was measured against this gitleaks pin rather than assumed. Only
# the commit already pushed to a public history is matched here.
#
# THE SAME CAUSE HAS A SECOND ENTRY, in .github/scan-accepted-fingerprints.txt
# BLOCK 4, keyed on the BLOB oid instead of the commit. That is not redundancy:
# this file governs the commit-attribution scan and that table governs the
# object scan, and the two fingerprint forms are not interchangeable — this one
# stops matching after a rebase, that one survives it.
